Initial technical inspection
Check what is appropriate:

file count, size, readability, archive integrity, format, delimiter, and encoding;
table or layer names, row and column counts, schema, and duplicate files;
expected time range, spatial extent, CRS, raster dimensions, alignment, and geometry validity;
sequence count and metadata linkage;
missing documentation and unexpected executable or unsupported content.

Do not perform substantive cleaning in the preserved source location.

Define the target
State exactly what quantity, comparison, prediction, pattern, or simulated outcome the analysis is intended to produce. The statistical term estimand may be used as a name for the quantity being estimated, but plain language is usually enough.
Record as applicable:

target population or system;
unit of observation, unit of analysis, and unit of inference;
outcome or response;
comparison or contrast;
prediction target or forecast horizon;
spatial and temporal support;
intended audience or decision;
uncertainty quantity to report.

Specify the method and settings
Describe the following in ordinary scientific language:

method or model family;
response, predictors, confounders, or adjustment variables;
interactions, offsets or denominators, and weights;
grouping, spatial structure, and temporal structure;
transformations and planned exclusions;
missing-data treatment;
consequential software or packages;
intended tables, figures, maps, forecasts, or simulation summaries.

Do not require mathematical notation or teach which method to choose.

Working-agreement topics
Record proportionately:

expected outputs and current phase;
contributor roles and decision process;
review and disagreement process;
expected response timing where consequential;
durable decision location;
change-proposal process;
version and file conventions;
access owner, data restrictions, and external-sharing restrictions;
required review types and record locations;
contribution-record location and credit discussion timing;
external partner contacts and conditions;
current owner, backup owner where warranted, and handoff trigger.

When communication work begins, identify the authoritative communication-product record and, when a release is consequential, the authoritative release record. Draft products belong to Communicate. Scientific changes return to Analyze or Validate; contributor or partner changes return to Collaborate; released versions and corrections remain visible in Communicate. Selected project history may proceed to the implemented Preserve stage, including when a product is unreleased or access is restricted.
Do not include credentials or protected information.

Assign roles and authority
Use roles rather than assuming a particular job title:

project lead;
data provider or steward;
analytical lead;
subject-matter reviewer;
technical reviewer;
operational owner;
release authority;
future maintainer.

One person may hold multiple roles. The purpose is to identify responsibility and decision authority, not to create unnecessary bureaucracy.






Example



A simulation project should fix scenario definitions, parameter ownership, acceptance criteria, and review authority before committing to a large HPC run. Otherwise a completed run may not answer the intended question.

Bayesian and sampling-based methods
Record and evaluate as appropriate:

chain behavior and convergence measures;
effective sample size;
divergent transitions or numerical warnings;
posterior predictive checks;
prior-to-posterior behavior;
sensitivity to initial values;
parameter identifiability.

A posterior predictive check compares observed data with data generated from the fitted model to see whether the model reproduces important features. Avoid universal convergence thresholds; use method-appropriate recommendations and record the thresholds actually applied.

Input and execution checks
Confirm as appropriate:

analysis ID and version;
direct input dataset IDs and versions;
code version and settings or parameter record;
random seeds, scenarios, replicate IDs, and partitions;
software and computing information;
run IDs;
expected outputs and outputs actually produced.

Recalculation and reruns
Use proportionately:

rerun a simple analysis from a clean starting point;
independently recalculate important values;
confirm table totals against source summaries;
compare maps with expected extents;
confirm deterministic work produces materially equivalent results;
confirm stochastic summaries are recoverable from recorded runs.

Exact bit-for-bit reproduction is not always reasonable when software, hardware, parallel execution, or numerical methods differ. Require material scientific agreement and document expected differences.

Recurring strengths
Across the sample, the most reusable strengths were:

a project-level README that states a research purpose or links to a paper;
R scripts grouped in a recognizable location;
rendered Quarto or HTML documentation for project outputs;
links to OSF or another external archive for large or supporting material;
explicit configuration files, manifests, workflow orchestration, or batch scripts in the more computationally intensive projects;
project-specific assets such as spatial layers, sequences, model objects, or rendered figures kept near the workflow that uses them;
demonstrable links between public code, supporting information, and publications in several projects.

These are observed practices, not proof that the underlying analyses are correct. The proposed standard treats purpose, provenance, traceability, and handoff as the common layer and leaves tools proportional to project needs.
